  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "One town's records say 130 children walked out and vanished. Hamelin, Germany. The date given is the 26th of June, 1284.",
        "visual_query": "lower saxony german town",
        "visual_fallback": "half timbered houses"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A stained-glass window went up in the church around 1300 showing it. A town entry from 1384 reads: it is 100 years since our children left.",
        "visual_query": "medieval stained glass window",
        "visual_fallback": "coloured glass"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Notice what is missing. No rats. No piper hired to clear them. The rat-catcher only enters the story around 1559 \u2014 nearly three centuries later.",
        "visual_query": "old german town square",
        "visual_fallback": "cobbled square"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So what actually took 130 children? The strongest evidence points east. Recruiters were pulling settlers out of German towns to colonise land in the east.",
        "visual_query": "eastern european farmland wagon",
        "visual_fallback": "open farmland"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Historians tracing Hamelin surnames found them scattered through Poland and Brandenburg. The children probably were not taken. They were hired.",
        "visual_query": "medieval european village street",
        "visual_fallback": "village lane"
      }
    ],
